WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.538
Endpoint organization affects security
review.

2
00:00:04.618 --> 00:00:11.347
Minimal APIs can grow from a few routes
into many endpoints across public features,

3
00:00:11.427 --> 00:00:17.388
tenant workflows, administrative
operations, internal integrations,

4
00:00:17.468 --> 00:00:21.025
health checks, and preview functionality.

5
00:00:21.105 --> 00:00:24.277
If those endpoints are mixed casually,

6
00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:31.086
reviewers can struggle to see which
security expectations apply to which surface.

7
00:00:31.166 --> 00:00:37.608
Route groups can apply shared conventions
and make related endpoints easier to

8
00:00:37.688 --> 00:00:38.457
inspect.

9
00:00:38.537 --> 00:00:43.439
A group can represent a public API area, a
tenant-scoped API,

10
00:00:43.519 --> 00:00:47.653
an administrative API, or a versioned API
surface.

11
00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:52.925
Shared metadata, tags, authorization
requirements, filters,

12
00:00:53.005 --> 00:00:58.100
and OpenAPI conventions can make the
group's purpose visible.

13
00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:04.909
Endpoint names, tags, route structure, and
metadata should communicate purpose.

14
00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:11.623
A route name that describes a business
operation is easier to review than a generic

15
00:01:11.703 --> 00:01:12.856
handler name.

16
00:01:12.936 --> 00:01:17.935
Tags help documentation readers understand
the API surface.

17
00:01:18.015 --> 00:01:24.745
Metadata can reveal authorization policy,
rate-limit policy, OpenAPI behavior,

18
00:01:24.825 --> 00:01:28.959
and other expectations that matter during
review.

19
00:01:29.039 --> 00:01:31.826
Admin, internal, public, tenant,

20
00:01:31.906 --> 00:01:37.867
and user-facing endpoints should not be
mixed without clear boundaries.

21
00:01:37.947 --> 00:01:44.292
A public route group should not casually
contain administrative operations.

22
00:01:44.372 --> 00:01:50.717
A tenant route group should preserve
tenant context and access expectations.

23
00:01:50.797 --> 00:01:57.430
Internal endpoints should have explicit
access rules and operational ownership

24
00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:02.317
rather than relying on where they happen
to live in the file.

25
00:02:02.397 --> 00:02:09.222
Group-level configuration improves
consistency, but it can also hide exceptions.

26
00:02:09.302 --> 00:02:15.743
If most endpoints in a group require one
policy and one endpoint needs different

27
00:02:15.823 --> 00:02:21.688
behavior, that exception should be
visible, documented, and reviewed.

28
00:02:21.768 --> 00:02:27.055
Exceptions are not forbidden, but they
should not be accidental.

29
00:02:27.135 --> 00:02:33.384
Good route organization also helps
authorization, OpenAPI documentation,

30
00:02:33.464 --> 00:02:35.579
logging, and operations.

31
00:02:35.659 --> 00:02:40.177
When endpoints are grouped by purpose and
sensitivity,

32
00:02:40.257 --> 00:02:46.698
teams can apply policies consistently,
produce more accurate documentation,

33
00:02:46.778 --> 00:02:53.700
create clearer metrics, and troubleshoot
production behavior with less guesswork.
